	(text "Pull down on the FAULT line is\nused to guarantee the behaviour\nat startup/reset time,\nguaranteeing that the Sensor\ncommunicates FAULT unless\nexplicitly set by the Sensor\nDevice.\nThis topology also ensures a\nFAULT condition when a Stuck\nOpen fault appens on the\nconnection wire"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20.574 273.812 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "110dd159-dbee-4780-ae2a-8aeb81fff457")
	)
	(text "No additional decoupling capacitor is needed on the supply line, as one is already present on the \nFlash module.\n\nMISO has a series resistor for source termination, which should be placed as close as possible to \nthe line driver. The resistor should only be used if ringing problems occur. Lowering the SPI \nclock frequency can also help reduce ringing effects (not the cause, which is reduced by the \ntermination resistor).\n\nCS has a pull-up resistor to keep the Flash deselected unless explicitly selected by the MCU. \nThis ensures a defined default state during power-up and reset."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 163.576 25.4 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "1f4b3be2-b358-430d-bc1c-72bfa19d1061")
	)
	(text "The SWD programming connector is separated from the main one \nbecause it is not actually part of the final system, and it is \nused only during testbench debug phase, while a full debugger can \nbe useful. When the system will be relased, tehe Firmware can be \nupdated from a bootloader running on CAN bus, without the need \nfor a full debugger"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 65.278 172.466 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "3146504c-7573-44ed-aef2-3abf4dd5a8ac")
	)
	(text "LEDs"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 265.938 15.24 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "35256ab6-d45e-46e2-aafc-17d256decc46")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\",\nwhich simulates a Fault on \nthe sensor lines. This \nconnectors can simulate Stuck-\nOpen Faults."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 53.086 271.526 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "3d2e0137-c9e7-45cc-88cc-0d1eac5bb8c6")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\", which \nsimulates a Stuck-Open Fault on \nCAN_H line"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 176.276 105.156 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "40543f9e-f718-46bd-9367-481e475e681c")
	)
	(text "CAN"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 146.304 61.468 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "411c9687-c96a-4f38-9a8a-b071b1be3e10")
	)
	(text "No hardware low-pass filter is used, so button debouncing must be handled in software. \nThe buttons are placed directly on the carrier PCB, so significant noise problems, \nwhich could have justified a filter, are not expected.\n\nExternal 47k pull-down resistors are used to guarantee a defined logic level even during \nMCU reset. The static Power consumption for each button is:\n\n	P = (3.3V)^2 / 47k = 0.23 mW"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 25.654 24.13 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "43265fa2-b24e-45d2-9078-3976c7dcd596")
	)
	(text "nRST circuit generates a signal that is normally \nHIGH (no reset), and LOW when button is pressed \n(reset). the capacitor is used to filter this \nsignal, and in particular avoid noise and EMI to \ntrigger the reset condition."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 260.858 56.896 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "4c475b76-7d2c-463f-905b-cfb5b1dc05f1")
	)
	(text "EMI Filtering"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 374.142 32.766 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "4d8f7b3d-7a86-4fa4-a65d-9edc3b9fdab7")
	)
	(text ""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 208.788 27.178 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 13.462 13.462)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5367050d-c49d-4c84-bcc2-35296222ef02")
	)
	(text "Display"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 121.158 15.24 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "55175726-e0ed-4302-8d44-599551141d98")
	)
	(text "VTRef"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 66.802 189.738 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "5ab6ea3a-7d68-49fc-8667-acef5b324336")
	)
	(text "The input supply voltage is monitored through ADC channel to detect UV and OV conditions. The voltage divider is designed to support the fault case in which two \n12 V batteries are placed in series (because of jump start mis-connection). A 30 V measurement range is selected to provide additional margin.\n\n    Volt. Divider Ratio = 3.3V / 30V = 0.11\n    --> Let's use R_bottom = 10k\n    --> R_top = 80.9k\n    --> E96 Series: R_top = 84.5k (to guarantee margin for resistor tollerances)\n\nLet's check the range\n    --> V_ADC_MAX = 30V * (10.1k / (83.655k + 10.1k)) = 3.232V\n    --> 3.23V < 3.3V --> 30V can be measured safely\n\nLet's check the resolution (in teh worst case, when the Voltage divider output is lower)\n    --> V_ADC_MIN = 30V * (9.9k / (85.345k + 9.9k)) = 3.118V\n    --> Resolution_WC = 3.3*(85.345+9.9)/9.9/4096 = 7.75 mV/LSB\n\nA 1 uF capacitor is placed across R_bottom in order to define an anti-aliasing filter and filter out of band components of the signal.\n\n    R_eq = (84.5k*10k)/(84.5k+10k) = 8.94 kOhm\n    --> f_cutoff = 1/(2*pi*8.94k*1uF) = 17.8 Hz\n\nStandard 1% resistors are selected since high-precision voltage measurements are not required for supply diagnostics, and so tollerance on the measured\nvoltage is acceptable.\n\n    --> Worst-case divider ratio error is approximately +/-1.8%, corresponding to about +/-0.43 V at 24 V, excluding ADC and reference voltage errors.\n\nThe accuracy can be improved through a one-time calibration using a known input voltage, defining a compensation factor for the actual resistor ratio."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 305.562 233.426 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5dad9e94-7394-4f9f-8532-ca778cfb8c34")
	)
	(text "Interlock"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 238.252 99.314 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "5eaea37e-4504-4057-9650-c5f7378660e8")
	)
	(text "The bus uses split termination with two 60.4 Ohm resistors and a 4.7nF capacitor \nconnected from their midpoint to GND. The two resistors provide approximately \n120 Ohm differential termination, while the capacitor creates a low-pass filtering\neffect for common-mode noise. \nDuring normal CAN communication, CANH and CANL move in opposite directions, so the \nmidpoint voltage remains approximately constant and the capacitor has\nlittle effect on the differential signal.\r\n\nA larger capacitor would provide stronger common-mode filtering, but could also \nintroduce problems under the fault situation when when one CAN line becomes \ndisconnected. In this case, the differential symmetry is lost and the capacitor \ncan affect the useful signal, introducing an RC delay that may prevent the signal \nfrom reaching the required voltage thresholds before the CAN sampling point. \nFor this reason, 4.7nF was selected instead of a much larger value, such as \n100nF, to obtain some common-mode filtering without unnecessarily \ndegrading signal integrity under fault conditions.\r\n\r\nThe termination resistors are sized considering possible short circuit fault to \nthe 12V main supply of one of the CAN lines, which should not result in fathal malfunctions.\n	P_MAX = (12V / (2*60.4))^2 * 60.4 = 0.596 W \n	--> Max power loss on each resitor\n	-->	Termination resistor should at least be rated for 1 W (and PCB should\n		be designed to handle the heat dissipation)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 140.716 78.74 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5efef19d-5fb0-4115-a514-333aa959cfd7")
	)
	(text "No common-mode choke is used, since no dedicated EMC testing is\nplanned to calibrate its parameters, guaranteeing an actual \nimprovement of the CAN performance."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.79 67.056 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "6462ecc3-d81a-4820-9a46-caad225fb8a6")
	)
	(text "Connectors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.536 161.036 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "670e4219-620f-4689-acba-1d81d02ad820")
	)
	(text "The chosen CAN transceiver uses a 5V supply for the bus interface \r\nand a 3.3V VIO supply for compatibility with the MCU logic levels.\r\nThe 5V supply provides more voltage headroom for the CAN output \r\nstage, making it easier to meet the required electrical and EMC \r\nperformance at a lower cost. In particular, it reduces the design\r\nconstraints on the internal circuitry compared to a transceiver \r\npowered entirely at 3.3V, reducing the component cost by a \r\nsignificant amount.\r\nAt the same time, the separate 3.3V VIO supply allows direct \r\ncommunication with the MCU without requiring additional level \r\nshifters."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.536 79.756 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "67ef01dc-98df-4418-88a1-af59119220f0")
	)
	(text "The series resistors are the termination resistors for the\nrespective SPI trasmission lines. In general, SPI do not need \ntermination from the standard, but because this connection is \nbetween two different boards, with un-definied cable length in a \nnoisy enviroment, the terminations are used to avoid reflections\nat high SPI frequencies, which can generate ringing \n(particularly critical on CLK line, where it can trigger \nmultiple clock edges. In other lines it is instead sufficient to \nslow down the SPI speed, to let the transmission line settle \nbefore sampling it). \nThese resistors must be mounted as close as possible to the \ndriver to be effective. \nThese terminations, if no ringing is observed during testing, \nshould be substitued with 0 Ohm resistors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 89.662 218.186 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "6929acc3-6aa3-415b-818e-4af2265e2e90")
	)
	(text "Main Voltage (12V) Monitor"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 330.454 212.344 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "6dee4d1f-4386-4024-bfbd-80fac6e838b9")
	)
	(text "Flash Memory"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 193.802 15.24 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "6f6c6997-e251-42c1-b08d-4d49f1ba6b4f")
	)
	(text "From LED datasheet the Typical Voltage drop at 5 mA is 2.95V.\nKnowing this, I can compute the series resistor:\n	R = (5V - V_forw_LED) / 5 mA = 410 Ohm --> (E12 series) R = 470 Ohm\n	P_loss = V * I = 5V * ((5V - 2.95V) / 470Ohm) = 21mW\n\nIf the Actual Forward Voltage of the LED is higher, it will simply light up with\nlower luminosity, so using the typical Forward Voltage value is ok (I don't need to \nbe precise on the actual current flowing in the LED, I just need it to light up)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 349.758 158.242 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "7392aaef-ef89-4f36-9f2c-417c89eedb14")
	)
	(text "Class D amplifier is chosen because the application (simple notification, warning and alarm sounds) do not need particular avoidance of distortion, and so\naffordability of the component and low power consumption was prioritized. \nThe nominal power of the amplifier with 8ohm loads (1.2W at 5V) is chosen to be greater than the speaker nominal power (1W). \nAs suggested by the datasheet, the DC component of the Input analog signal is filtered by a series capacitor.\nThe Amplifier is supplied at 5V in order to guarantee that the maximu power output is reachable (with 3V3, the output power would have been impaced because \nideally: Pmax = Vdd^2 / (2*R))\nThe nShutdown pin has a Pull-Down to guarantee the behaviour of the amplifier in Reset conditions (amplifier is shut down unless explicitly waken up by the\nMicrocontroller)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 306.578 24.13 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "7652e487-b9ae-4655-8a6c-4c6861bcacaa")
	)
	(text "Speaker"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 355.092 15.494 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "8381f684-bc1b-4b9e-a8e1-f404fed6a3e1")
	)
	(text "Mounting Holes M3"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 230.378 133.604 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.016 1.016)
			)
		)
		(uuid "88239632-87b9-4321-b85e-01f7f379b334")
	)
	(text "UART has a separated connector because it is not actually part of \nthe system, and is used only for debugf porpuses"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 65.786 195.834 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "88cd8583-9acc-47ec-93bd-2ead1edbd867")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\", which \nsimulates a Stuck-Open Fault on \nCAN_L line"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 176.276 125.476 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "940d7cf7-7c63-4d8d-80bf-3ddaf5b81010")
	)
	(text "The Human Side requires two main voltage levels: 5V and 3.3V. The 5V rail supplies the CAN\ntransceiver and the Steering Wheel Sensor PCB, where local LDOs generate a stable 3.3V supply \nfor the analog circuitry. The 3.3V rail powers the Human Side\n electronics, including\nsignificant loads such as the display and LEDs.\n\nBoth rails are generated directly from the main 12V vehicle supply using DC/DC buck \nconverters, chosen for their high efficiency and reduced heat dissipation.\n\nAlthough 3.3V could be generated from the 5V rail through an LDO, a dedicated buck \nconverter was preferred due to the significant current consumption and possible \nfuture expansions. This \nalso avoids the additional losses associated with a cascaded power supply."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 276.098 108.458 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "9a70fef7-9653-45b0-b617-6716509b3a55")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation \nConnector\", which \nremoves power \nsupply to \nthe Sensor Board"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 111.506 242.824 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "9d4abb51-e91d-43c5-9a26-b1580deb732e")
	)
	(text "No additional decoupling capacitor is needed on the supply line, as one is already present on the \nDisplay module.\n\nCS has a pull-up resistor to keep the Display deselected unless explicitly selected by the MCU.\nThis ensures a defined default state during power-up and reset."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 88.9 22.352 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "a6412abb-5ec8-4ffe-a958-9c5e6463aa15")
	)
	(text "Debug Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 85.344 166.37 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b0246dfd-fe56-441d-861a-ebd7b7783bda")
	)
	(text "User Buttons"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 50.546 14.986 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
