	(text "Main Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 50.038 198.628 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b6f01156-f285-4ce0-9817-1500c4c21044")
	)
	(text "A Filter is placed on each amplifier output to attenuate high-frequency noise generated by the switching elements, while preserving the audio signal.\nBecause of the very low impedance of the Speaker (8 Ohm), an RC filter is not raccomanded, because of the non negligible power loss on the resistor. \nA feerite bead is used insted, because it guardantees a very small DC and low frequency resistance (reducing the power dissipation of the filter), while having \na significant impedance at high frequency, implementing the filtering operation. \n\nThe low frequency dissipation is (assuming DC resistance specified in the datasheet is also valid in audio band, up to 10kHz):\n	P_loss = Ploss = Irms^2*(2*0.04) = 10 mW\n	--> Losses are negligible in comparison to speaker consumption\n\nThe DC current rating of the ferrite (3A) is chosen in accordance to peak current consumed by the Speaker (500 mA), with some margin to not run into \nthermal problems.\n\nAs noted in the TI reference \"TI_Audio Amplifier Output Filtering\", the exact values of the filter elements (ferrite and capacitors) are defined empirically,\nin order to address the real response and behavior of the circuit on hand. \nAs first guess, the guidelines of this document and amplifier datasheet are followed, chosing a ferrite with impedance > 100 Ohm at high frequency (it was chosen\none with 300Ohm @ 100 MHz), and the capacitor value of 220pF. \n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 306.324 81.026 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "bb5dd9be-7a15-4eeb-be31-43aca71ee36e")
	)
	(text "Inductor is chosen following the rules in the comment below:\n    - DC Resistance <= 140 mOhm\n    - I_SAT >= 1.9 A (Design peak: 1.264 A, ~50% margin)\n    - I_RMS >= 1.25 A (Design RMS: 0.819 A, ~50% margin)\n    - Preferably shielded"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 272.288 144.272 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "bbd151a5-43f6-48f7-9c54-0cf3fa36dbae")
	)
	(text "TVS diode is used to improve the ESD strike resistance of the \nCAN tranciver. It allows to withstand automotive standard ISO \n10605 30kV stikes, in comparison to the 8kV strikes for which \nthe CAN tranciver is rated. \n\nTVS diode must be placed as close as possible the CAN connector \ntoprotect efficiently the CAN transceiver. A direct connection \nto wide GND plane is required to disspate efficiently energy \nfrom the ESD strike with a low impedance return path. \n\nThe TVS is bidirectional to proect against ESD strikes with both \npolarities. It has a 24V working voltage (V_RWM) to avoid \nconducting during normal operations and during possible \nsustained overvoltage conditions (1 CAN line is shorted to 12V \nMain Supply). \nThe clamping voltage (V_CL), the voltage reached during a strike \nis 33V, which is less than the maximum absolute rating of the \ntranciver CAN pins of 42V, ensuring that the strike won't \nactually damage the tranciver.\nThe capacitance of 31pF added on each CAN line is small, \nbasically not degrading the dynamic perfomance of the CAN.\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 123.19 136.652 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "c39ea5f7-9663-4f07-a55f-84e33e547e46")
	)
	(text "Reset Button"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 266.192 49.784 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "dfdf9eb8-bbcf-4b28-b230-5e8b4f05bf4e")
	)
	(text "Mechanicals"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 238.252 115.824 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "e23f8e03-5a85-4550-95b8-74f29c72067c")
	)
	(text "From LED datasheet the Typical Voltage drop at 5 mA is 2.95V.\nKnowing this, I can compute the series resistor:\n	R = (3.3V - V_forw_LED) / 5 mA = 70 Ohm --> (E12 series) R = 82 Ohm\n	P_loss = V * I = 3.3V * ((3.3V - 2.95V) / 82Ohm) = 14mW\n\nIf the Actual Forward Voltage of the LED is higher, it will simply light up with\nlower luminosity, so using the typical Forward Voltage value is ok (I don't need to \nbe precise on the actual current flowing in the LED, I just need it to light up)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 351.79 201.93 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "e733c966-a018-489d-88e2-7c57ce85a4ef")
	)
	(text "Fiducials"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 249.936 133.604 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.016 1.016)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f228151f-f4ea-4e09-9020-2365f4c53a40")
	)
	(text "The Inductor proposed by the TI design was not available, so a new one, compatible with the DCDC circuit \nrequirements is selected using the following constraints\n\n	- DC resistance: <= 150 mOhm (to maintain or improve the DCDC efficiency)\n	- Saturation Current: From the report, the max current on L is 827.37 mA	\n		--> I_str >= 1.5A (to guarantee some margin)\n	- RMS Current: Considering the current on L as a triangular wave (it is a semplification), the RMS current is computed as:\n		--> I_RMS = sqrt(I_OUT^2 + (Delta_I_L^2 / 12)) = sqrt(0.5^2 + (0.65475^2 / 12)) = 0.535 A\n		--> I_RMS >= 1 A (to have some margin)\n	- L should be preferrably shielded to reduce EMI emissions"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 273.558 197.612 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "f34a0f37-bc97-44b4-ad02-b48957a92e24")
	)
	(text "Power Supply Interface"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 42.164 166.37 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f385156e-d16e-4f6f-8556-8cf9d24a9530")
